# Host-global analysis-platform configuration.
#
# Sourced, never executed. One Dom0 has one analysis bridge, one uplink,
# one VLAN address space and one volume group, so these values have
# exactly one correct setting per host -- not one per target OS.
#
# Every consumer resolves its own location first and sources this file
# alongside its per-OS one:
#
#     SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")" && pwd)"
#     . "$SCRIPT_DIR/platform.env"
#     . "$SCRIPT_DIR/pipeline-ubuntu.env"
#
# Values are written as ${NAME:-default}, so anything already set in the
# environment wins and CI can override without patching this file.
#
# --- Why this file exists (issue #6, decision 2) ------------------------
#
# Three copies of pipeline-<os>.env would mean three places that must
# agree about the bridge name, with nothing checking that they do. Two
# pipelines disagreeing about ANALYSIS_BRIDGE is not a misconfiguration
# that fails loudly -- it is a clone attached to a bridge with no
# isolation rules on it, which looks like a working analysis.
#
# C4 in docs/architecture/analysis-pipeline.md therefore forbids
# pipeline-<os>.env from defining any name below, and the check is a
# grep:
#
#     grep -nE '^(VG_NAME|ANALYSIS_BRIDGE|UPLINK_BRIDGE|SUBNET_PREFIX)' \
#         automation/pipeline-*.env      # expect: no output
#
# The test for whether a value belongs here is not "how many scripts read
# it" but "would two OS pipelines ever be right to disagree". CLONE_LV_SIZE
# stays per-OS by that test -- Android's 32G against Ubuntu's 64G is a
# property of the guest image. MAX_CLONES deliberately stays in
# pipeline-<os>.env despite being host-global, because DRAFT-036 replaces
# it with memory-based admission outright and moving it twice is worse
# than moving it once.
#
# --- What this file does NOT fix ----------------------------------------
#
# Setting a value here does not rename it everywhere. xenbr1 is still a
# literal in all four misc/*.cfg files, which are Xen configs and cannot
# source shell. Changing ANALYSIS_BRIDGE below without editing those
# leaves clones attached to the old bridge. The .cfg literals are
# deliberately out of scope: a Xen config is not shell, and the clone
# script rewrites the vif line anyway.

# --- Networking ---------------------------------------------------------

# The analysis bridge. VLAN sub-interfaces <bridge>.<vlan> are created on
# it, one per clone slot, and every isolation rule matches on the
# `<bridge>.+` wildcard rather than on anything guest-specific.
#
# This script does not create it -- `ovs-vsctl add-br xenbr1` is a
# one-time install step, and network-setup.sh fails with a message naming
# that step if the bridge is absent.
ANALYSIS_BRIDGE="${ANALYSIS_BRIDGE:-xenbr1}"

# The uplink to the Internet. Clone egress is narrowed to this interface:
# traffic out of a VLAN sub-interface to anywhere other than the uplink is
# dropped, which is what stops clone-to-clone and clone-to-LAN traffic.
UPLINK_BRIDGE="${UPLINK_BRIDGE:-xenbr0}"

# Size of the host-global VLAN pool: VLANs 1..TOTAL_VLANS are provisioned
# by network-setup.sh and leased by dirwatch (DRAFT-036).
#
# Host-global by the file's own test -- "would two OS pipelines ever be
# right to disagree". They would not: there is one set of VLAN
# sub-interfaces on one bridge, and two watchers disagreeing about how many
# exist is exactly the collision this replaced. MAX_CLONES stays per-OS and
# now means only "how many analyses may this watcher run at once"; the VLAN
# it uses is no longer its slot index.
#
# MUST BE >= the sum of every concurrently-running watcher's MAX_CLONES, or
# watchers block waiting for a lease that a busy neighbour holds. dirwatch
# refuses to start if it is below its own MAX_CLONES, which catches the
# single-watcher case but cannot see the others -- the sum is an operator
# responsibility until admission becomes memory-based.
#
# Every launcher passes this to network-setup.sh instead of its own clone
# count, so provisioning matches the pool rather than whichever pipeline
# started last.
TOTAL_VLANS="${TOTAL_VLANS:-8}"

# First two octets of the per-VLAN gateway scheme, <prefix>.<vlan>.1/24.
# The third octet IS the VLAN id, which is why network-setup.sh rejects a
# clone count above 255.
#
# This is NOT the RFC1918 range used in the isolation drop list. That list
# covers 172.16.0.0/12 among others and is about what a clone may reach,
# not about where its own gateway lives; the two are independent and the
# drop list stays hard-coded.
SUBNET_PREFIX="${SUBNET_PREFIX:-172.16}"

# --- Storage ------------------------------------------------------------

# LVM volume group holding the golden images, the sandbox templates and
# every per-clone copy-on-write snapshot.
#
# Read by clone.pl from the environment rather than from this file
# directly -- it is Perl, invoked by dirwatch on a fixed 3-argument
# contract, so run_analysis-<os>.sh exports this for it.
VG_NAME="${VG_NAME:-vg-root}"